Template: partman-scubed3/commit_failed
Type: error
Description: The partition table could not be written
 An error occurred while writing the changes to the disks. scubed3 is not
 configured.

Template: partman-scubed3/no_math64
Type: error
Description: The shell of the installer has no 64 bit arithmetic
 partman-scubed3 needs it to compute the sizes of the blocks. scubed3
 cannot be configured with this installer.

Template: partman-scubed3/no_base
Type: error
Description: No base device for scubed3
 No partition is used as the base device of scubed3. Choose a partition,
 and set "Use as:" to "base device for scubed3".

Template: partman-scubed3/too_many_bases
Type: error
Description: More than one base device for scubed3
 The installed system can use only one base device of scubed3. Use only one
 partition as "base device for scubed3".

Template: partman-scubed3/too_large
Type: error
Description: ${DEVICE} is too large for scubed3
 scubed3 cannot use this partition as its base device. Use a smaller
 partition (at most some tens of terabytes).

Template: partman-scubed3/options
Type: string
Description: Options of the scubed3 daemon:
 The sizes of the blocks are not stored on the base device: the same -m
 (mesoblock size) and -M (macroblock size) must be used forever. The
 default is chosen for the size of the base device. Change this only if you
 know what you are doing, for example to use an existing base device with
 the sizes it was made with.

Template: partman-scubed3/store_type
Type: select
Choices-C: new, existing
Choices: A new scubed3 store (the partition is filled with random data), An existing scubed3 store (keep the data on the partition)
Default: new
Description: What is on ${DEVICE}?
 scubed3 hides encrypted partitions in a device full of random data:
 without its passphrase a partition cannot be told apart from unused space.
 .
 A new store starts with random data on the whole partition. If the
 partition already holds scubed3 partitions, choose the existing store: you
 need its salt (in /etc/scubed3.conf of the system that made it) and the
 passphrases of ALL its partitions.

Template: partman-scubed3/fill
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Fill ${DEVICE} with random data?
 All data on ${DEVICE} will be overwritten with random data. This is the
 last opportunity to abort.
 .
 This can take long on a large partition. Without it, the old data on the
 partition shows which blocks scubed3 has written, and which not: do not
 skip it, unless the partition was filled with random data before.

Template: partman-scubed3/progress/fill_title
Type: text
Description: Filling ${DEVICE} with random data

Template: partman-scubed3/progress/fill_text
Type: text
Description: The installer is now overwriting ${DEVICE} with random data. Cancelling leaves the rest of the partition as it is.

Template: partman-scubed3/fill_failed
Type: error
Description: ${DEVICE} could not be filled with random data
 An error occurred while ${DEVICE} was filled with random data. scubed3 is
 not configured.

Template: partman-scubed3/no_salt
Type: error
Description: No random salt
 The installer could not make a random salt for scubed3.

Template: partman-scubed3/salt_note
Type: note
Description: The salt of the new scubed3 store
 The passphrases of the scubed3 partitions are turned into keys with this
 salt:
 .
 ${SALT}
 .
 It is not secret, but without it the partitions cannot be opened. It is
 stored in /etc/scubed3.conf of the installed system (and in its initramfs);
 also keep a copy elsewhere, on paper is fine.

Template: partman-scubed3/salt
Type: string
Description: Salt of the existing scubed3 store:
 The salt is in /etc/scubed3.conf of the system that made the store (the
 line salt=...). Without the right salt no partition can be opened. Leave it
 empty for the default salt of scubed3 (if that system had no salt= line).

Template: partman-scubed3/iterations
Type: string
Default: 16777216
Description: Number of iterations of the key derivation:
 The line iterations=... of /etc/scubed3.conf of the system that made the
 store; 16777216 is the default of scubed3.

Template: partman-scubed3/start_failed
Type: error
Description: scubed3 could not be started
 The scubed3 daemon did not start on the base device.
 .
 ${ERROR}

Template: partman-scubed3/mainmenu
Type: select
Choices-C: create, open, finish
Choices: Create a partition, Open an existing partition, Finish
Description: scubed3 configuration actions
 Open partitions: ${PARTITIONS}
 .
 Create the partitions you want now, also hidden ones you will not install
 on. On an existing store, open ALL its partitions before you finish: in
 paranoia level 2 and 3 scubed3 overwrites the blocks of partitions that
 are not open.
 .
 To install on a partition, it needs LUKS on it (the LUKS volume is the
 disk you partition next, with the root file system on it).

Template: partman-scubed3/name
Type: string
Description: Name of the scubed3 partition:
 The name is used for the file of the partition, and for its LUKS volume
 (/dev/mapper/scubed3-NAME). It is not stored on the base device: you can
 use another name every time you open it. Letters, digits, '.', '_' and
 '-' only.

Template: partman-scubed3/bad_name
Type: error
Description: Invalid name
 A name has at most 30 characters: letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-'; it
 does not start with a '.'.

Template: partman-scubed3/name_exists
Type: error
Description: "${NAME}" is already open
 A partition with this name is open already. Choose another name.

Template: partman-scubed3/no_space
Type: error
Description: No unclaimed blocks
 All blocks of the base device belong to partitions.

Template: partman-scubed3/size
Type: string
Description: Size of the scubed3 partition "${NAME}":
 The space for data, for example "20 GB" (at most ${MAX}; a quarter more is
 reserved for the garbage collector). Partitions can be enlarged and
 shrunk later with scubed3ctl, the file system on them must be resized
 with them.
 .
 Installing goes fastest when the partition you install on has most of the
 blocks of the base device. Do not make it larger than you need for that:
 at the end of the menu, the blocks that no partition claims are lent to
 it during the installation, and given back afterwards.

Template: partman-scubed3/bad_size
Type: error
Description: Invalid size
 The size is not valid, or larger than the unclaimed space.

Template: partman-scubed3/luks
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Put LUKS on "${NAME}"?
 LUKS on the partition protects its data when someone takes the computer
 while it runs (in paranoia level 2 and 3 all partitions are open then).
 The installer can only install on a partition with LUKS on it: the LUKS
 volume is the disk you partition next. Its passphrase is asked when the
 menu is finished; use another passphrase than the one of the partition.

Template: partman-scubed3/passphrase
Type: password
Description: Passphrase:
 The passphrase of ${DESCRIPTION}.
 .
 With the wrong passphrase scubed3 finds no blocks, as if the partition
 does not exist. Choose a passphrase that is hard to guess.

Template: partman-scubed3/passphrase-again
Type: password
Description: Re-enter the passphrase to verify:
 Please enter the same passphrase again to verify that you have typed it
 correctly.

Template: partman-scubed3/passphrase-mismatch
Type: error
Description: Passphrase input error
 The two passphrases you entered were not the same. Please try again.

Template: partman-scubed3/passphrase-empty
Type: error
Description: Empty passphrase
 You entered an empty passphrase, which is not allowed. Please choose a
 non-empty passphrase.

Template: partman-scubed3/weak_passphrase
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Use weak passphrase?
 You entered a passphrase that consists of less than eight characters,
 which is considered too weak. You should choose a stronger passphrase.

Template: partman-scubed3/create_failed
Type: error
Description: The partition "${NAME}" could not be created
 ${ERROR}

Template: partman-scubed3/no_blocks
Type: error
Description: No blocks found
 No blocks of "${NAME}" were found with this passphrase: the passphrase is
 wrong, or the salt is not the salt of this store (or the partition does
 not exist: scubed3 cannot tell the difference).

Template: partman-scubed3/level
Type: select
Choices: 1, 2, 3
Default: 2
Description: Paranoia level during the installation:
 Level 1: the writes of a partition go to its own blocks (this shows which
 blocks belong to which partition, to someone with two copies of the
 disk, or with the write log of a flash drive).
 .
 Level 2: every write goes to a random block of the whole base device.
 ALL partitions on the base device must be open now: a partition that is
 not open is destroyed.
 .
 Level 3: as level 2, and the writes are done at random moments, also
 when nothing is written; a partition writes at most its share of the rate
 (800 KiB/s by default), installing takes long.
 .
 Level 2 is recommended for the installation.

Template: partman-scubed3/boot_level
Type: select
Choices: 1, 2, 3
Default: 2
Description: Paranoia level of the installed system:
 The initramfs of the installed system switches to this level after it has
 opened the partitions (the line paranoia= of /etc/scubed3.conf). In level
 2 and 3 the names and passphrases of ALL partitions on the base device are
 asked at boot.

Template: partman-scubed3/level_failed
Type: error
Description: The paranoia level could not be switched
 ${ERROR}

Template: partman-scubed3/lend
Type: select
Choices: ${CHOICES}
Description: Lend the unclaimed blocks during the installation to:
 ${FREE} of the base device belong to no partition. In paranoia level 2 and
 3, a partition with a fraction f of the blocks of the base device needs on
 average 1/f block writes for a block of its own data (a partition with a
 tenth of the blocks: ten), and the garbage collector copies less when a
 partition has more free blocks. So installing is much faster on a
 partition that has most of the blocks.
 .
 The blocks are lent: the size of the partition does not change, the
 blocks are extra room for the garbage collector. At the end of the
 installation they are given back, and the garbage collector of the
 installed system removes them from the partition in the background, while
 it writes (scubed3ctl p shows "shrinking" until it is done). Then they can
 be used for other (hidden) partitions.
 .
 Choose the partition you install on.

Template: partman-scubed3/lend_failed
Type: error
Description: The blocks could not be lent to "${NAME}"
 ${ERROR}

Template: partman-scubed3/luks_failed
Type: error
Description: LUKS could not be put on "${NAME}"
 ${ERROR}

Template: partman-scubed3/luks_open_failed
Type: error
Description: The LUKS volume on "${NAME}" could not be opened
 The passphrase is wrong, or the LUKS header is damaged.

Template: partman-scubed3/not_configured
Type: error
Description: scubed3 is not configured
 A partition is used as the base device of scubed3, but scubed3 has not
 been configured. Choose "Configure scubed3" in the main menu, or use the
 partition for something else.

Template: partman-scubed3/only_root
Type: error
Description: Only the root file system can be on scubed3
 The installed system sets up only the root partition at boot, "${MOUNTPOINT}"
 cannot be on a scubed3 partition. Put it on the root file system, or on a
 normal partition. Other scubed3 partitions can be used after the
 installation, with scubed3ctl.

Template: partman-scubed3/no_swap
Type: error
Description: No swap on scubed3
 The kernel writes swap through the scubed3 daemon, which needs memory to
 do that. Do not put swap on a scubed3 partition; use swap on zram, or no
 swap.

Template: partman-scubed3/root_needs_boot
Type: error
Description: No separate /boot partition
 The root file system is on scubed3, the kernel and the initramfs must be
 on a /boot partition that is not on scubed3. Create one (about 1 GB).

Template: partman-scubed3/install_failed
Type: error
Description: scubed3 could not be set up in the installed system
 The step "${WHAT}" failed, see the installation log. The installed system
 may not boot from its scubed3 partition.

Template: partman-scubed3/store_root_name
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Store the name of the root partition on the boot partition?
 The root file system is on the scubed3 partition "${NAME}". If its name is
 stored in the initramfs (on the unencrypted boot partition), it is not
 asked at boot; anyone who has the disk can read it. If it is not stored,
 you type it at boot, before its passphrase, and you can have more than one
 root partition (for example one you can admit and a hidden one).

Template: partman-scubed3/final_note
Type: note
Description: scubed3 is set up
 Keep a copy of /etc/scubed3.conf (the salt: ${SALT}) and of the options of
 the daemon (${OPTIONS}): without them the partitions cannot be opened.
 .
 The blocks that were lent to the partition you installed on are given
 back: the garbage collector removes them while the system writes.
 "scubed3ctl p" shows "shrinking" until it is done. After that they can be
 used for other partitions.
 .
 At boot, the names and passphrases of all scubed3 partitions are asked
 (in paranoia level 2 and 3 ALL of them must be opened), then the
 passphrase of the LUKS volume of the root partition. A key store can
 replace all of them by one passphrase, see "Key store" in the README of
 scubed3.
